== WHAT IT IS
    exported c fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Rejected:
        if n < 0:
            fail Rejected {code: n}
        n * 2

An exported function is an ordinary function: its Botlish callers, every
analysis and every backend see an ordinary block. Building the program as
a native library gives it a C entry point too:

    botlish -emit-native-library calc -library-dir out calc.bot

writes out/libcalc.a, out/libcalc.so, out/calc.h and out/calc.json. The C
function is calc_checked (LIBRARY_NAME), or the symbol the declaration
states: `exported c fn checked named "calc_check"(n: int) -> int`.
-export FUNCTION exports a top-level function that is not declared so.

== THE C INTERFACE
    calc_instance *in;
    calc_error *err;
    calc_create(&in, &err);                 /* one instance per caller */
    int64_t out;
    int32_t status = calc_checked(in, -5, &out, &err);
    /* status == calc_DECLARED_ERROR; calc_error_identity(err) is
       "Rejected", field 0 is code = -5 */
    calc_error_release(err);
    calc_destroy(in);

Parameter and result types, each declared: `int` is int64_t (an Int
result outside it is a BOUNDARY error, never truncated), `bool` is C's
bool, `Float64` a finite double, `str` UTF-8 bytes (a pointer and a length
in, validated; an owned NAME_bytes out, released with NAME_bytes_release),
`unit` a result with no result parameter. Every call returns a status: OK,
DECLARED_ERROR, RUNTIME_ERROR, BOUNDARY_ERROR or INVOCATION_ERROR, and an
error object the caller owns, with its identity, message and payload as
data.

== RULES
  * An exported function declares every parameter's type and its result
    type, each with a C representation (error.export-signature); it is
    environment-free (error.export-capture); its symbol is unique
    (error.export-symbol).
  * Each export is compiled as an independent root: for every value its
    declared types admit, never for what its Botlish callers happen to
    pass.
  * The library's root file declares and runs nothing: creating an
    instance initializes the modules it imports, and a top-level statement
    is refused (error.native-library).
  * An instance belongs to the thread that created it; every call comes
    from that thread. Instances, and libraries, share no state. Botlish
    code runs on the caller's stack: recursion that would exhaust it is the
    runtime error NATIVE LIMIT STACK, never a crash.
  * Linux glibc (x86_64, aarch64) only; no callbacks, collections, structs
    or asynchronous calls cross the boundary yet.

@example accept
exported c fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

twice(21)
@caption An exported function is an ordinary function to every backend
@value 42
@end

@topic error.export-signature
title: EXPORT-SIGNATURE: an exported function without a C signature
summary: An `exported c fn` declares every parameter and its result with a type that has a C representation (int, bool, Float64, str; unit for the result) and no flag, context, variadic or resume section.
index: errors
alias: exported function signature; no C representation; export needs declared types
keywords: exported, c, export, parameter, result, declared, int, bool, Float64, str, unit, context, flags, variadic, yield, native library
related: concept.native-library error.export-capture error.export-symbol
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SIGNATURE

== MEANING
A C caller passes C values: the function's C signature is its declared
Botlish signature at the boundary, never one inferred from its Botlish
callers. So every parameter and the result are declared, each with one of
the types that have a C representation:

    int      int64_t           bool     bool
    Float64  double (finite)   str      const uint8_t *, size_t (UTF-8)
    unit     (a result only: no result parameter)

Also refused: a flag section, a context parameter or a required context
(no native library installs one), a variadic parameter, a resume clause,
and a function that may yield.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the types (`fn f(n: int) -> int`). A value of another type crosses
as one of these: a List as a str the C side parses, a refined Int as an
int the function checks.

@example reject
exported c fn f(x) -> int:
    1

0
@caption A parameter without a declared type
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SIGNATURE
@at 1:16
@end

@topic error.export-capture
title: EXPORT-CAPTURE: an exported function that captures a binding
summary: An `exported c fn` is environment-free: it may call other functions and read module bindings, never a binding of its file's top level, which a native library never runs.
index: errors
alias: exported function captures; export is not environment-free
keywords: exported, capture, closure, top level, module binding, environment, native library
related: concept.native-library error.export-signature concept.modules
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md MODULE-STATIC-RETAINED-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-CAPTURE

== MEANING
A native library never runs its root file's statements, so a binding made
there would have no value when a C caller calls the export. Functions and
module bindings (a module's top-level bindings, initialized when an
instance is created) are fine.

== WHAT TO DO
Make the value a function, or move it into a module the file imports.

@example reject
limit = 3

exported c fn f(x: int) -> int:
    x + limit

0
@caption The export reads a top-level binding of its own file
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-CAPTURE
@at 3:16
@end

@topic error.export-symbol
title: EXPORT-SYMBOL: two exported functions with one C symbol
summary: Two `exported c fn` declarations state the same C symbol (`named "SYMBOL"`); a library's C symbols are unique.
index: errors
alias: duplicate export symbol; C symbol exported twice
keywords: exported, named, symbol, duplicate, C, native library
related: concept.native-library error.export-signature error.native-library
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SYMBOL

== MEANING
A stated C symbol names one function of the library. (A symbol the
library derives, LIBRARY_NAME, is checked when the library is built:
NATIVE LIBRARY SYMBOL.)

== WHAT TO DO
State another symbol for one of them.

@example reject
exported c fn f named "same"(x: int) -> int:
    x

exported c fn g named "same"(x: int) -> int:
    x

0
@caption One C symbol, two functions
@error CORE SEMANTIC EXPORT-SYMBOL
@at 4:29
@end

@topic error.export-syntax
title: EXPORT-SYNTAX: a malformed exported c fn declaration
summary: `exported c fn NAME [named "SYMBOL"](PARAMS) -> TYPE:` at the top level of a module is the one export form; anything else is a located syntax error.
index: errors
alias: exported function syntax; exported fn without c; malformed exported c fn
keywords: exported, c, fn, named, symbol, syntax, nested
related: concept.native-library error.extern-syntax error.surface-syntax
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: EXPORT-SYNTAX
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | EXPORT-SYNTAX: *

== MEANING
The parser reports, at the offender: an export without its ABI
(`exported fn`), another ABI than c, `named` on a function that is not
exported, a symbol that is not a C identifier, and an export nested in a
function, loop or branch.

== WHAT TO DO
Write `exported c fn NAME(...) -> T:` at the top level of a file, with
`named "SYMBOL"` after the name when the C symbol should differ from the
library's default.

@example reject
exported fn f(n: int) -> int:
    n

0
@caption The ABI is missing
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:10
@end

@topic error.native-library
title: NATIVE LIBRARY: building a native library failed
summary: NATIVE LIBRARY errors refuse a native library build: no exports, a name that is no C identifier, a root file with statements, a C symbol taken twice, an unsupported host, a missing tool.
index: errors backends
alias: native library build failed; library mode error
keywords: -emit-native-library, -export, -library-dir, native::library, ld, objcopy, ar, cc, symbol, top level, host
related: concept.native-library error.export-signature error.native-aot-not-ready error.native-not-built
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md
code: NATIVE LIBRARY

== MEANING
At build time (-emit-native-library, native::library); nothing is written.
The codes:

    NATIVE LIBRARY EXPORT     no exported function, or -export names no
                              top-level function of the input
    NATIVE LIBRARY NAME       the library's name is no C identifier
    NATIVE LIBRARY TOP-LEVEL  the root file has a statement, which would
                              never run (instance creation initializes only
                              the modules it imports)
    NATIVE LIBRARY SYMBOL     two exports, or an export and the library's
                              own API (NAME_create, ...), share a C symbol
    NATIVE LIBRARY HOST       not Linux glibc on x86_64 or aarch64
    NATIVE LIBRARY TOOL       ld, objcopy, ar or cc is missing or failed
    NATIVE LIBRARY OUTPUT     no output directory was given
    NATIVE LIBRARY ROOT       the input is not a program
    NATIVE LIBRARY INPUT      the input is core IR text

== WHAT TO DO
Move top-level statements into functions, or data into a module; state a
`named` symbol for a clash; install GNU binutils and a C compiler driver.
A program whose code needs run-time kind checks is NATIVE AOT NOT-READY,
as for an executable.
